 do that  the question is how it's done and it  ought to be done with respect and with  care for the people who you're employing  um we did hear the prime minister um  talk about the peerless british civil  service when he made his speech that  we'll call a resignation speech last  week but he once tried to cut what 91  000 jobs in the civil service over the  cost of living crisis um  how important is it  to have a prime minister that is um duly  respectful of the staff around him well  it's very important um i always think of  gerald ratner you remember i do very  much and you don't get give leadership  you don't get loyalty you don't get  results if you don't treat your  employees with respect  when we talk about the civil service um  people at home this morning getting  ready for work were thinking what does  the civil service actually do and the  answer is you know that all sorts of  things they don't just work in offices  in whitehall here do they they work in  prisons they work all over the place yes  and it's their job to support the  government of the day to support its  policies and to carry them out as  efficiently as they can  and they are roles that are um  non-political that must be quite tricky  on occasion well of course i mean  government is about politics but you  mustn't take a partisan view so what  civil servants have to do is to work for  the government that the electors  produce  how difficult is the transition not only  from when you have one prime minister of  the same colour to another one uh you  know whoever the next conservative prime  minister will be but also when you have  a complete change of regime well it can  be a bit of a personal trauma yes i'm  sure so tell me more about that yeah  well you get very close to the person  you're working with i got very close to  john major and then tony blair came in  and then you just have to demonstrate  that you are going to do your best for  the new incumbent as professionally as  you can and 